WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.870
A lot goes into making a movie — especially
one in the sprawling Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:08.600
— and sometimes directors don't get things
quite right on the first go-around.

00:00:08.769 --> 00:00:12.860
Such is the case for Avengers: Endgame and
filmmakers Joe and Anthony Russo, who brought

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.560
back Tilda Swinton to reshoot her scene more
than a year after she wrapped on it.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.000
That's right: We haven't exhausted every possible
Endgame topic!

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.540
"I understood that reference."

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.460
A movie of this magnitude is chock full of
behind-the-scenes secrets.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.280
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.720
Swinton, who plays the Ancient One, appears
during a pivotal moment in the middle of Endgame.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.770
When Professor Hulk and company travel back
in time to 2012 New York City to retrieve

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.010
three Infinity Stones as part of their time
heist mission, the green guy pays the Ancient

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.760
One a visit at the Sanctum Sanctorum and asks
her to surrender the Time Stone.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.440
While Ant-Man, Captain America, and Iron Man
are off retrieving the Space and Mind Stones,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.100
Professor Hulk is on the receiving end of
a lecture about how one can't just take an

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.220
Infinity Stone out of its proper place without
severely damaging the existing timeline.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.840
It's an important scene that establishes how
time travel works in the MCU – but it's

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.320
a scene that originally played out differently.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.080
Chatting with IndieWire, Swinton revealed
that the Russo Brothers called her into film

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.220
the sequence all over again a year after she
first shot it.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.220
Swinton explained,

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.800
"I went back to reshoot it because a couple
of lines had been changed, a couple of plot

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.840
points had been changed.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.500
And there was a tweaking of my costume."

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.120
As is expected, Swinton couldn't dish up exact
details on what the original take entailed,

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.940
though she did state that the reworked plot
points were "all about" the visual timeline

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.659
the Ancient One crafts when explaining to
Professor Hulk the rules of time travel and

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.640
alternate realities.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.670
She then noted that the alterations centered
around, quote, "the information that the Ancient

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.250
One holds about the nature of the [Infinity]
Stones, that they have to be put back."

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.530
In the theatrical cut of the film, the Ancient
One goes into great detail about how the timeline

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.580
is impacted when someone takes an Infinity
Stone from one point in time and transports

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.460
it to another.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.460
That kind of interdimensional tomfoolery creates
a branch timeline that is dark, chaotic, and

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.500
doesn't abide by the same laws that the original
timeline does.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:57.840
This explanation informs the Avengers' decision
to have one team member — Captain America

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.140
— return the Stones after they defeat Thanos
at the end of the film.

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:05.340
Swinton also said that the Russo Brothers
changed up the dialogue in her scene to better

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.420
connect to Endgame shooting down time travel
rules presented in movies like Back to the

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.860
Future and Bill &amp; Ted's Excellent Adventure.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:12.980
"What?

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:14.740
How?”

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.060
“Good tea."

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.370
Swinton also gushed about her experience working
on Endgame, and said that the double shoot

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.400
was more than worth it.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.279
"That was amazing, and a whole other world
of filmmaking.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:25.780
But, boy, was it worth it.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.900
They tweaked it into something extraordinary."

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.320
"Time travel!"

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.400
We may never know what the original version
of the Sanctum Sanctorum sequence in Endgame

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.360
was like, but it sounds like the reshoot added
some much-needed clarity to the film.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.580
Fans have had a hard time wrapping their heads
around how time travel works within the context

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.820
of the MCU in general …

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:43.900
"It's a dumb question."

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:44.800
"Okay.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.300
Got it.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.300
So we all agree it's possible.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.100
Now what?"

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.900
… and how time travel works in Endgame specifically,
even with the reshoot, so it's a good thing

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.200
the Russos summoned Swinton back to the set.

